00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:24.590
You don't have to pay for it.

00:11:24.590 --> 00:11:28.280
And in the Colab notebooks
you can write your code,

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:30.170
you can execute
the code, and you

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:32.265
can see the output, all
within one notebook.

00:11:32.265 --> 00:11:34.640
And it's very convenient for
sharing with your teammates,

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:36.080
for example.

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:39.110
OK, a second step
in the data pipeline

00:11:39.110 --> 00:11:43.310
is we're going to write model
input features into what

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:44.830
is called TFRecord files.

00:11:44.830 --> 00:11:48.870
So this is the standard format
for presenting input features

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:51.800
data for TensorFlow models.

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:55.720
The third step is we're going
to launch model training using

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:59.020
Cloud ML engine.

00:11:59.020 --> 00:12:03.690
When the training job
starts, the workers

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:07.720
will be loading model features
from TensorFlow record

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:10.330
files that have been
previously written

00:12:10.330 --> 00:12:12.580
to Google Cloud storage.

00:12:12.580 --> 00:12:14.920
And periodically,
the worker jobs

00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:18.730
will be saving model state
checkpoints, as well as

00:12:18.730 --> 00:12:21.370
training and
evaluation statistics

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:24.500
in the bucket in cloud storage.

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:26.720
So that's the pipeline.

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:29.379
Now, let's take a look
at model predictions.

00:12:29.379 --> 00:12:30.795
What we're going
to be predicting,

00:12:30.795 --> 00:12:31.990
let's start with that.

00:12:31.990 --> 00:12:34.510
What metric will the
model be forecasting?

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:37.750
When we're talking about stock
price, forecast, and model,

00:12:37.750 --> 00:12:41.540
the key is to define what
horizon we're talking about.

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:44.770
Are we going to be
forecasting for a year?

00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:45.770
For several months?

00:12:45.770 --> 00:12:46.600
Days?

00:12:46.600 --> 00:12:50.230
So to be specific, for
this example that we're

00:12:50.230 --> 00:12:53.135
going to show you, we're going
to be forecasting intraday day

00:12:53.135 --> 00:12:56.020
stock returns with a
five-minute horizon.

00:12:56.020 --> 00:12:57.890
The reason we chose
this for this example

00:12:57.890 --> 00:13:00.190
is because this is
something useful--

00:13:00.190 --> 00:13:00.730
practical.

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:03.010
It's useful for
scheduling suborders

00:13:03.010 --> 00:13:05.710
for algorithmic
execution orders--

00:13:05.710 --> 00:13:07.090
execution algos.

00:13:07.090 --> 00:13:09.670
And it's useful for
intraday trading strategies.

00:13:09.670 --> 00:13:13.090
So it's something relevant.

00:13:13.090 --> 00:13:16.450
Now, let's take a big
picture view for a moment

00:13:16.450 --> 00:13:19.600
and discuss a little
bit what exactly we're

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:22.070
going to be doing here.

00:13:22.070 --> 00:13:24.530
We're going to be using
supervised learning,

00:13:24.530 --> 00:13:27.730
meaning that we're going to be
training our model on examples.

00:13:27.730 --> 00:13:33.010
Each training example is a
pair comprising input features

00:13:33.010 --> 00:13:34.910
and the correct answer.

00:13:34.910 --> 00:13:37.150
So input features
are whatever inputs

00:13:37.150 --> 00:13:40.300
the model is going to be using
to give us their prediction.

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:41.840
And the label is
the correct answer.

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:43.820
So given inputs and
correct answers,

00:13:43.820 --> 00:13:48.520
the model will be learning,
and learning until it hopefully

00:13:48.520 --> 00:13:51.940
is able to generate correct
or more or less correct

00:13:51.940 --> 00:13:54.790
predictions, even when it
doesn't know what they're going

00:13:54.790 --> 00:13:56.160
to be based on the inputs.

00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:01.230
So what are going
to be our labels?

00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:04.390
How do we construct the
labels-- predictions?

00:14:04.390 --> 00:14:07.160
So our goal is to
predict intraday returns

00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:09.400
for a five-minute horizon.

00:14:09.400 --> 00:14:12.150
And for this, we need
to define the starting

00:14:12.150 --> 00:14:14.292
price and the final
price-- the end price.

00:14:14.292 --> 00:14:15.750
For the starting
price, we're going

00:14:15.750 --> 00:14:18.000
to be using the average
bid/ask midpoint

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:19.920
for the current
10-second interval.

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:21.420
And for the final
price, we're going

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:24.490
to be using five-minute
volume weighted

00:14:24.490 --> 00:14:27.480
average price, or a VWAP,
for the five-minute interval

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:30.250
subsequent to the
current point in time.

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:33.180
And that is actually
relevant, because when

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:37.230
you schedule
suborders, you'll want

00:14:37.230 --> 00:14:39.780
to know what will be the VWAP.

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:42.720
Not just some price at
some random point in time,

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:45.984
but VWAP may be
more relevant here.

00:14:45.984 --> 00:14:48.400
So this is the picture that
illustrates what exactly we're

00:14:48.400 --> 00:14:48.900
doing.

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:52.000
We are calculating
the average midpoint,

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:56.380
we're calculating the VWAP for
the next five-minute interval.

00:14:56.380 --> 00:14:59.310
And we take the logarithm
of those two values.

00:14:59.310 --> 00:15:01.420
And the difference of
logarithms is the label

00:15:01.420 --> 00:15:05.270
that we're going to be using
for training the model.

00:15:05.270 --> 00:15:07.730
So after we're done with
building and training

00:15:07.730 --> 00:15:10.250
the model, we're
going to be taking

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:12.470
a look at evaluations
of this to see

00:15:12.470 --> 00:15:14.180
how well the model performs.

00:15:14.180 --> 00:15:17.000
But I guess you're already
curious to see how it performs,

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:19.280
and so we're going to have
dessert first before we're

00:15:19.280 --> 00:15:21.570
done with the main entry.

00:15:21.570 --> 00:15:22.070
OK.

00:15:22.070 --> 00:15:23.400
So this is what we did.

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:25.820
For this example,
we trained the model

00:15:25.820 --> 00:15:28.280
on 16 days of Thomson
Reuters market data

00:15:28.280 --> 00:15:30.560
between May 1st and 24th.

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:34.520
And we evaluate that model
on one day of market data--

00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:35.510
May 25th.

00:15:35.510 --> 00:15:38.150
So you can see that
evaluation data should always

00:15:38.150 --> 00:15:40.530
be after the end of
the training data.

00:15:40.530 --> 00:15:44.150
Don't want to have
any look-aheads here.

00:15:44.150 --> 00:15:47.447
And we're using R
square as a measure

00:15:47.447 --> 00:15:48.530
of the quote of the model.

00:15:48.530 --> 00:15:49.238
What is R square?

00:15:49.238 --> 00:15:52.730
Approximately, it
tells you how good

00:15:52.730 --> 00:15:55.310
is the model compared
to a trivial model that

00:15:55.310 --> 00:15:56.850
always predicts zero return.

00:15:56.850 --> 00:15:59.550
So for this particular
example, R square

00:15:59.550 --> 00:16:03.770
was 0.1, which means that
the model was useful.

00:16:03.770 --> 00:16:06.770
If R square is zero, it means
the model is as good or as bad

00:16:06.770 --> 00:16:09.950
as the trivial model that
always predicts zero returns.

00:16:09.950 --> 00:16:12.620
If R square is 1, it means
the model is perfect.

00:16:12.620 --> 00:16:14.365
If it's between 0 and
1, it means that it

00:16:14.365 --> 00:16:16.560
has some predictable ability.

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:20.830
So here, you can see that for
the zero prediction model,

00:16:20.830 --> 00:16:24.530
the mean squared error
on evaluation data

00:16:24.530 --> 00:16:27.980
was 231 squared basis
points, where a basis

00:16:27.980 --> 00:16:30.500
point is one hundredth, or 1%.

00:16:30.500 --> 00:16:35.300
And the mean squared error for
our model on evaluation data

00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:36.320
was 207.

00:16:36.320 --> 00:16:39.920
So it was slightly better
than the trivial model.

00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:42.080
And this is roughly
what would be expected.

00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:47.240
Because you do not realistically
expect to forecast all or even

00:16:47.240 --> 00:16:50.270
most of the variance at
five-minute intervals,

00:16:50.270 --> 00:16:54.340
because there is a lot of
noise in intraday prices.

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:57.850
So let's take a look at
how we build the inputs.

00:16:57.850 --> 00:17:02.140
What are we going to be
feeding into the model?

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:05.829
We're going to be defining
input features based

00:17:05.829 --> 00:17:09.940
on what types of factors
that affect stock

00:17:09.940 --> 00:17:13.390
returns we're trying
to capture here.

00:17:13.390 --> 00:17:17.819
So first, we're going to
learn intraday price patterns

00:17:17.819 --> 00:17:19.069
at different times of the day.

00:17:19.069 --> 00:17:22.300
And to do this, we're going
to include a feature based

00:17:22.300 --> 00:17:24.940
on the intraday sequence
number of the current 10-second

00:17:24.940 --> 00:17:27.099
interval.

00:17:27.099 --> 00:17:30.320
Then we want to capture mean
reversion or momentum price

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:30.887
patterns.

00:17:30.887 --> 00:17:33.345
And for this, we're going to
be using a list of differences

00:17:33.345 --> 00:17:38.900
of logarithms of average
midpoint quotes in the last 120

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:39.770
10-second intervals.

00:17:39.770 --> 00:17:42.920
So essentially what this
means, this long sentence,

00:17:42.920 --> 00:17:46.520
is that we're looking
at returns between

00:17:46.520 --> 00:17:49.640
consecutive 10-second
intervals for the trailing 20

00:17:49.640 --> 00:17:51.330
minute window.

00:17:51.330 --> 00:17:55.230
And we're using this list as
one of the input features.

00:17:55.230 --> 00:17:58.047
Then we want to capture
price patterns for stocks

00:17:58.047 --> 00:17:59.130
at different price levels.

00:17:59.130 --> 00:18:00.963
And for this, we're
looking at the logarithm

00:18:00.963 --> 00:18:07.160
of the price at the
current 10-second interval.

00:18:07.160 --> 00:18:09.320
Volume is also relevant.

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:11.030
So as one of the
input features, we're

00:18:11.030 --> 00:18:15.320
going to be using volume in
the trailing 20 minute window

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:17.120
preceding the current interval.

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:19.900
But for this to
be useful, we want

00:18:19.900 --> 00:18:22.880
to normalize the volume by the
stock's average daily volume

00:18:22.880 --> 00:18:26.730
over some reasonably
long time range.

00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:29.450
And for example, we used
4-week average daily volume

00:18:29.450 --> 00:18:30.630
as a normalization factor.

00:18:30.630 --> 00:18:35.400
So we take traded volume for
this stock in the last 20

00:18:35.400 --> 00:18:39.020
minutes, and we divided by these
stock's average daily volume

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:42.440
on the past four weeks.

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:44.930
And finally, we're going to be
using an input feature based

00:18:44.930 --> 00:18:47.600
on their stock's
security identifier, RIC,

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:50.760
because different stocks may
have their unique distinct

00:18:50.760 --> 00:18:52.040
intraday price patterns.

00:18:52.040 --> 00:18:56.030
And I will show how to plug
in a RIC or string in general

00:18:56.030 --> 00:18:56.780
into the model.

00:18:59.530 --> 00:19:03.640
Now get ready for some heavy
construction work ahead.

00:19:03.640 --> 00:19:05.140
I'm going to start
showing you some

00:19:05.140 --> 00:19:08.680
slides with the actual
SQL code snippets.

00:19:10.957 --> 00:19:13.040
So first of all, we're
going to be using BigQuery.

00:19:13.040 --> 00:19:14.240
What is BigQuery?

00:19:14.240 --> 00:19:17.370
It's Google Cloud's
enterprise data warehouse,

00:19:17.370 --> 00:19:18.690
ideal for analytics.

00:19:18.690 --> 00:19:21.300
You can run standard
SQL queries on it.

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:23.220
And also, it has some
very useful extensions--

00:19:23.220 --> 00:19:24.030
analytic functions.

00:19:24.030 --> 00:19:26.760
I will show how to
use those functions.

00:19:26.760 --> 00:19:28.320
It's fully managed
and serverless,

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:31.320
meaning that Google takes
care of provisioning of CPU

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:33.845
and storage capacity
of data application.

00:19:33.845 --> 00:19:37.900
You don't have to worry
about any of that.

00:19:37.900 --> 00:19:40.120
Feature number one--
intraday sequence number

00:19:40.120 --> 00:19:41.780
of the current
10-second interval.

00:19:41.780 --> 00:19:44.470
So what I want to
do here is basically

00:19:44.470 --> 00:19:47.320
calculate the number of
seconds since midnight

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:48.760
and divide it by 10.

00:19:48.760 --> 00:19:53.560
And to do this, we extract
the hour, multiply by 360.

00:19:53.560 --> 00:19:56.620
We convert from UTC
time into local time,

00:19:56.620 --> 00:19:59.450
because we care
about stock patterns

00:19:59.450 --> 00:20:03.460
specific to the exchanges
local time, not UTC time.

00:20:03.460 --> 00:20:06.130
We extract number of
minutes multiplied by 6,

00:20:06.130 --> 00:20:10.700
and we extract number of seconds
divided by 10, and round it up.

00:20:10.700 --> 00:20:14.705
So this is our interval
sequence number.

00:20:14.705 --> 00:20:16.080
Building block
for feature number

00:20:16.080 --> 00:20:18.720
two-- list of 10-second
interval midpoints.

00:20:21.540 --> 00:20:24.640
So one nice thing
BigQuery provides

00:20:24.640 --> 00:20:29.650
is the ability to use temporary
tables as part of the queries.

00:20:29.650 --> 00:20:33.560
So in this example, we
use interval midpoints

00:20:33.560 --> 00:20:34.580
as a temporary table.

00:20:34.580 --> 00:20:36.920
You write a query, and
the output of the query

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:39.140
is stored in the
temporary table.

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:44.930
And then we can use this table
from within another query.

00:20:44.930 --> 00:20:48.120
And here, we're using
array aggregation function,

00:20:48.120 --> 00:20:51.170
which is an analytic
function of BigQuery that

00:20:51.170 --> 00:20:59.540
allows you to apply a certain
function over a series of rows

00:20:59.540 --> 00:21:01.770
preceding each
row in the output.

00:21:01.770 --> 00:21:07.252
So what I want to do here
is to partition data by RIC.

00:21:07.252 --> 00:21:10.220
And the OVER clause
specifies how exactly

00:21:10.220 --> 00:21:11.450
we're going to do it.

00:21:11.450 --> 00:21:15.170
So we'll partition data by
RIC, by stock identifier.

00:21:15.170 --> 00:21:17.750
Within each partition,
we'll order rows

00:21:17.750 --> 00:21:20.380
by interval sequence number.

00:21:20.380 --> 00:21:27.560
And with this ordering for each
row, we take 120 preceding rows

00:21:27.560 --> 00:21:29.630
and make a list of
them, and return these

00:21:29.630 --> 00:21:31.280
as the output of the query.

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:37.131
Feature number three--
normalize traded volume.

00:21:37.131 --> 00:21:38.380
So there are three steps here.

00:21:38.380 --> 00:21:39.910
As I mentioned
before, we compute

00:21:39.910 --> 00:21:43.330
each stock's average daily
volume for the last four weeks.

00:21:43.330 --> 00:21:47.980
We compute interval volumes,
normalize them by ADV.

00:21:47.980 --> 00:21:50.740
And then for each interval,
we compute the sum

00:21:50.740 --> 00:21:54.790
of normalized volumes for
those 10-second intervals

00:21:54.790 --> 00:21:56.540
in the preceding
20-minute window.

00:21:56.540 --> 00:21:58.360
So there are three steps here.

00:21:58.360 --> 00:22:01.690
And similar as before,
we use a temporary table

00:22:01.690 --> 00:22:05.700
to store average daily volumes.

00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:10.140
Then we use this temporary
table from another query

00:22:10.140 --> 00:22:17.090
to calculate interval
vols normalized by ADV.

00:22:17.090 --> 00:22:21.050
And then we use those values
from yet another query

00:22:21.050 --> 00:22:23.290
in which we use an analytic
function called SUM.

00:22:23.290 --> 00:22:26.890
Which instead of making
a list of overall values

00:22:26.890 --> 00:22:29.350
from preceding rows, here
it just adds up values

00:22:29.350 --> 00:22:30.910
from a list of preceding rows.

00:22:30.910 --> 00:22:32.470
And as before, we
specify that we

00:22:32.470 --> 00:22:37.265
want to specify that we want
the partition data by RIC,

00:22:37.265 --> 00:22:39.730
we want to order the
rows in each partition

00:22:39.730 --> 00:22:42.250
by interval sequence
number, and for each row,

00:22:42.250 --> 00:22:47.650
we want to apply the sum
for 120 preceding rows.

00:22:47.650 --> 00:22:51.020
Now I'm going to show how
to construct a building

00:22:51.020 --> 00:22:51.920
block for the label--

00:22:51.920 --> 00:22:56.000
VWAP in the five-minute period
subsequent to each interval.

00:22:56.000 --> 00:22:58.430
This is something
that we won't have

00:22:58.430 --> 00:23:01.100
when we're generating
forecasts, but this is something

00:23:01.100 --> 00:23:04.440
that we're going to have
when training the model.

00:23:04.440 --> 00:23:06.440
So once again, we
use a temporary table

00:23:06.440 --> 00:23:08.810
for interval price volumes.

00:23:08.810 --> 00:23:12.590
We use this table
from another query,

00:23:12.590 --> 00:23:16.250
which for each row
for each interval,

00:23:16.250 --> 00:23:21.190
computes the sum
of price volumes,

00:23:21.190 --> 00:23:22.930
and then divides by
the sum of volumes.

00:23:25.860 --> 00:23:30.420
Now, one thing that's very
convenient about using BigQuery

00:23:30.420 --> 00:23:33.560
for data and Google Cloud
is that it's very nicely

00:23:33.560 --> 00:23:35.120
integrated with Python.

00:23:35.120 --> 00:23:41.060
There is a pandas package
called io.gbq that has

00:23:41.060 --> 00:23:42.650
this method called read gbq.

00:23:42.650 --> 00:23:45.220
You can pass the query,
you pass your project ID

00:23:45.220 --> 00:23:48.980
in Google Cloud, and it
returns the output of a query

00:23:48.980 --> 00:23:50.555
into a pandas dataframe.

00:23:50.555 --> 00:23:52.430
And then it can do
additional transformations

00:23:52.430 --> 00:23:54.980
of data generation
of final features

00:23:54.980 --> 00:24:00.020
from our semi-final
results of queries

00:24:00.020 --> 00:24:01.880
directly within pandas
dataframes, which

00:24:01.880 --> 00:24:03.260
have very rich functionality.

00:24:03.260 --> 00:24:05.600
So it's very convenient.

00:24:05.600 --> 00:24:09.650
Now, once we have this final
dataframe with all features

00:24:09.650 --> 00:24:14.030
that you need, you can
save it into a JSON file,

00:24:14.030 --> 00:24:16.220
with to JSON function.

00:24:16.220 --> 00:24:19.430
JSON format is very convenient
in that it preserves

00:24:19.430 --> 00:24:21.540
the structure of data.

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:24.770
So for example, it will
save lists as lists.

00:24:24.770 --> 00:24:27.100
If you used to CVS
function, for example,

00:24:27.100 --> 00:24:29.150
it will save lists
as strings and you

00:24:29.150 --> 00:24:30.440
will lose the structure.

00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:32.650
So I recommend to JSON.

00:24:32.650 --> 00:24:38.910
And then finally, if you
wish, you can use gsutil tool.

00:24:38.910 --> 00:24:40.610
It's one of the
tools in Google Cloud

00:24:40.610 --> 00:24:44.570
to copy your JSON file into one
of the buckets in the Cloud.

00:24:48.670 --> 00:24:51.260
Generating TFRecord files.

00:24:51.260 --> 00:24:53.590
So a TFRecord is the
recommended format

00:24:53.590 --> 00:24:57.080
for storing serialized model
features in TensorFlow.

00:24:57.080 --> 00:25:00.900
So what we do, we run queries
into queries to get that data,

00:25:00.900 --> 00:25:03.000
we'll put data into
dataframes, we'll

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:05.050
apply additional
transformations.

00:25:05.050 --> 00:25:09.400
And then we use those dataframes
with everything prepared

00:25:09.400 --> 00:25:11.330
to generate TFRecord files.

00:25:11.330 --> 00:25:16.190
And those files will be later
fed to the model for training.

00:25:16.190 --> 00:25:18.650
So there are three steps here.

00:25:18.650 --> 00:25:21.020
Generating training examples
comprising model input

00:25:21.020 --> 00:25:22.370
features.

00:25:22.370 --> 00:25:25.490
Serializing training
samples into TFRecord files.

00:25:25.490 --> 00:25:29.470
And feeding TFRecord
files into the model.

00:25:29.470 --> 00:25:30.980
So what is a training example?

00:25:30.980 --> 00:25:33.090
It's actually a Python
dictionary or a map.

00:25:33.090 --> 00:25:37.057
It maps feature names feature
protos, so protocol messages.

00:25:37.057 --> 00:25:38.140
And there are three types.

00:25:38.140 --> 00:25:40.680
You can have a list of
bytes, a list of floats,

00:25:40.680 --> 00:25:41.850
or a list of integers.

00:25:41.850 --> 00:25:43.330
You will always have a list.

00:25:43.330 --> 00:25:46.590
And what do you do with the
features that are scalars.

00:25:46.590 --> 00:25:48.630
If it's on a list,
you simply have a list

00:25:48.630 --> 00:25:51.450
with the one element in it.

00:25:51.450 --> 00:25:53.340
So that is an example.

00:25:53.340 --> 00:25:55.020
There is how we
construct an example--

00:25:55.020 --> 00:25:57.870
assuming that each
role holds values

00:25:57.870 --> 00:25:59.610
for features from
our dataframe that we

00:25:59.610 --> 00:26:00.651
have constructed earlier.

00:26:03.270 --> 00:26:06.360
So delta logs VWAP mid--
this is what we're going

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:07.500
to be using for our label.

00:26:07.500 --> 00:26:10.320
This is the correct answer that
we only have during training.

00:26:10.320 --> 00:26:12.570
We don't have it when we're
actually using the model

00:26:12.570 --> 00:26:14.910
to make predictions.

00:26:14.910 --> 00:26:18.270
And we have five features
that I described earlier.

00:26:18.270 --> 00:26:21.240
The RIC, stock identifier,
interval sequence number,

00:26:21.240 --> 00:26:24.810
deltas of log mids, sum
of interval volumes,

00:26:24.810 --> 00:26:26.320
and logarithm of
the current mid.

00:26:29.760 --> 00:26:33.880
How do you use these to actually
generate TFRecord files?

00:26:33.880 --> 00:26:38.930
The first step is to
initialize the TFRecord writer.

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:42.290
Then you iterate over
rows in the dataframe

00:26:42.290 --> 00:26:45.020
that contains the features.

00:26:45.020 --> 00:26:48.140
And for each row, you
construct an example

00:26:48.140 --> 00:26:50.810
based on the feature
dictionary that I showed you

00:26:50.810 --> 00:26:52.730
on the previous slide.

00:26:52.730 --> 00:26:55.820
Then you serialize this
example and you write it

00:26:55.820 --> 00:26:58.860
into the TFRecord writer.

00:26:58.860 --> 00:27:01.430
Finally, when you are done,
you're closing the writer.

00:27:01.430 --> 00:27:06.590
And you have this file
with serialized TFRecords.

00:27:06.590 --> 00:27:10.010
If you only intend to
train your model locally,

00:27:10.010 --> 00:27:13.310
you can generate this
file in a local directory.

00:27:13.310 --> 00:27:16.550
But if you are planning
to use Cloud ML Engine,

00:27:16.550 --> 00:27:20.360
you have to copy these TFRecords
file into Cloud Storage

00:27:20.360 --> 00:27:24.375
so that it can be accessed by
multiple jobs, multiple tasks

00:27:24.375 --> 00:27:27.410
that are running as part
of the Cloud ML Engine job.

00:27:30.960 --> 00:27:34.420
Preparing data to
train the model.

00:27:34.420 --> 00:27:38.520
So TFRecords can be efficiently
fed into TensorFlow models

00:27:38.520 --> 00:27:39.870
for training and evaluation.

00:27:39.870 --> 00:27:41.610
And in the next
several slides, we're

00:27:41.610 --> 00:27:44.460
going to look at
exactly how we're

00:27:44.460 --> 00:27:48.760
going to be feeding those
models to our model.

00:27:48.760 --> 00:27:50.670
And there are three steps here.

00:27:50.670 --> 00:27:53.850
The first step is we're going
to define feature columns.

00:27:53.850 --> 00:27:57.330
And I will show you in
a moment what they are.

00:27:57.330 --> 00:28:00.240
Second step, we're going
to create a parsing spec.

00:28:00.240 --> 00:28:03.600
And third step, we're going to
define an input function that

00:28:03.600 --> 00:28:07.050
will use the parsing spec.

00:28:07.050 --> 00:28:10.770
Step one, defining feature
columns for the model.

00:28:10.770 --> 00:28:14.220
So a feature column is a list.

00:28:14.220 --> 00:28:18.730
It specifies the
type of each feature.

00:28:18.730 --> 00:28:20.370
So here, for
example, for the RIC,

00:28:20.370 --> 00:28:24.090
we're using what's called
an embedding column.

00:28:24.090 --> 00:28:27.900
We're plugging stocks
identifier, a string,

00:28:27.900 --> 00:28:29.400
as an embedding.

00:28:29.400 --> 00:28:32.840
Which means essentially,
that we're making the string

00:28:32.840 --> 00:28:37.290
into a numeric value.

00:28:37.290 --> 00:28:39.580
And this numeric
value may represent,

00:28:39.580 --> 00:28:42.180
for example, a stocks propensity
to have mean-reverting

00:28:42.180 --> 00:28:45.280
versus momentum price patterns.

00:28:45.280 --> 00:28:49.420
And the one caveat that
you need to be aware of

00:28:49.420 --> 00:28:52.150
is when they use
embeddings, you want

00:28:52.150 --> 00:28:55.082
to make sure that
you set hash bucket

00:28:55.082 --> 00:28:56.290
size to a large enough value.

00:28:56.290 --> 00:28:59.350
Because you certainly
want to avoid collisions

00:28:59.350 --> 00:29:02.110
in the hash map used from
embeddings as much as possible.

00:29:02.110 --> 00:29:04.026
Because essentially,
when you have collisions,

00:29:04.026 --> 00:29:06.607
the model doesn't distinguish
between two different RICs

00:29:06.607 --> 00:29:08.440
in this case, and you
don't want to do that.

00:29:11.070 --> 00:29:16.890
Then you have feature columns
for other model inputs.

00:29:16.890 --> 00:29:18.840
In this case, interval
sequence number,

00:29:18.840 --> 00:29:21.360
sum of interval volumes
for the trailing window,

00:29:21.360 --> 00:29:23.310
and logarithm of
the current mid.

00:29:23.310 --> 00:29:25.850
You can see that we
specified their shape.

00:29:25.850 --> 00:29:28.860
The first dimension
is one in all cases.

00:29:28.860 --> 00:29:31.590
Meaning that they
all have with one--

00:29:31.590 --> 00:29:33.450
they're all scalars.

00:29:33.450 --> 00:29:39.720
And then we have delta log mids,
for which we specify shape,

00:29:39.720 --> 00:29:41.690
with the first
dimension set to 120.

00:29:41.690 --> 00:29:43.740
Reflect on the fact
that it's expected

00:29:43.740 --> 00:29:49.550
to be at least 120 values.

00:29:49.550 --> 00:29:51.480
Step two here is
creating parsing spec.

00:29:51.480 --> 00:29:55.070
And this is very easy to
do with a function called

00:29:55.070 --> 00:29:57.650
make parse example spec.

00:29:57.650 --> 00:30:00.860
So you pass this list of
feature specifications

00:30:00.860 --> 00:30:02.330
for this function,
and it gives you

00:30:02.330 --> 00:30:06.900
a feature of spec that we can
use in the input function.

00:30:06.900 --> 00:30:08.241
Which is the next step.

00:30:08.241 --> 00:30:09.740
We're going to
define input function

00:30:09.740 --> 00:30:11.390
in the next couple of slides.

00:30:11.390 --> 00:30:13.310
Let's see what we're doing here.

00:30:13.310 --> 00:30:16.189
The first step is
constructing a dataset.

00:30:16.189 --> 00:30:18.230
And for this, I recommend
using a function called

00:30:18.230 --> 00:30:20.330
make batched features dataset.

00:30:20.330 --> 00:30:23.360
It creates all-ready
performance optimized dataset

00:30:23.360 --> 00:30:25.250
with batching and
shuffling built-in.

00:30:25.250 --> 00:30:28.540
So you don't need to worry about
reading your data efficiently.

00:30:28.540 --> 00:30:30.465
You don't need to
worry about setting up

00:30:30.465 --> 00:30:32.840
multiple threads to read your
data because sometimes it's

00:30:32.840 --> 00:30:34.190
a bottleneck.

00:30:34.190 --> 00:30:35.670
It does everything for you.

00:30:35.670 --> 00:30:39.140
So where previously you would
make several function calls,

00:30:39.140 --> 00:30:42.390
here you can just
use this function.

00:30:42.390 --> 00:30:44.600
So let's look at
the parameters here.

00:30:44.600 --> 00:30:50.630
The file pattern specifies
the list of TFRecord

00:30:50.630 --> 00:30:52.460
files that you have
generated previously.

00:30:52.460 --> 00:30:54.860
So for example, you can
have a separate TFRecord

00:30:54.860 --> 00:31:00.970
file for market data from
this thing-- the trading base.

00:31:00.970 --> 00:31:02.030
Then batch size.

00:31:02.030 --> 00:31:04.107
So here, we'll set it 128.

00:31:04.107 --> 00:31:04.940
What does this mean?

00:31:04.940 --> 00:31:07.270
It means that the
training examples

00:31:07.270 --> 00:31:10.470
are going to be passed through
the model not one by one,

00:31:10.470 --> 00:31:12.760
but all together in batches.

00:31:12.760 --> 00:31:17.080
And each batch is going to
have 128 training examples.

00:31:17.080 --> 00:31:20.010
When you feed training
examples through the model,

00:31:20.010 --> 00:31:22.900
it generates predictions.

00:31:22.900 --> 00:31:25.690
And the lowest is computed
for the difference

00:31:25.690 --> 00:31:28.210
between predictions and
actual correct answers.

00:31:28.210 --> 00:31:32.350
And then based on the
lowest, the back propagation

00:31:32.350 --> 00:31:34.730
of gradients is applied.

00:31:34.730 --> 00:31:36.730
So when you use a batch,
the back propagation

00:31:36.730 --> 00:31:39.280
happens only once
within the entire batch.

00:31:39.280 --> 00:31:42.029
All gradients are averaged
over all training examples,

00:31:42.029 --> 00:31:44.070
so then there is back
propagation happening once.

00:31:44.070 --> 00:31:45.670
So this is called mini-batching.

00:31:45.670 --> 00:31:49.390
And usually it improves
model performance

00:31:49.390 --> 00:31:53.940
with the batch size set around
a hundred to a thousand.

00:31:53.940 --> 00:31:58.880
Now, the feature spec is
what we constructed earlier.

00:31:58.880 --> 00:32:00.650
Number of epochs is set to one.

00:32:00.650 --> 00:32:03.530
This simply means that we're
reading the dataset just once.

00:32:03.530 --> 00:32:06.740
We can read it several
times if you wish.

00:32:06.740 --> 00:32:08.630
Shuffle is set it true.

00:32:08.630 --> 00:32:12.470
What this does, it
randomizes the order

00:32:12.470 --> 00:32:17.270
in which it puts training
examples that it reads

00:32:17.270 --> 00:32:20.220
from the file into batches.

00:32:20.220 --> 00:32:23.310
It makes the model more robust.

00:32:23.310 --> 00:32:24.700
Then we make an iterator.

00:32:24.700 --> 00:32:26.370
So make one shot
iterator, it simply

00:32:26.370 --> 00:32:30.840
creates an iterator that reads
the data from the dataset

00:32:30.840 --> 00:32:31.572
just once.

00:32:31.572 --> 00:32:33.030
There are other
types of iterators.

00:32:33.030 --> 00:32:36.030
Some of them read
data more than once.

00:32:36.030 --> 00:32:37.410
And this is interesting.

00:32:37.410 --> 00:32:38.432
The get next function.

00:32:38.432 --> 00:32:40.390
So it looks like, if you
look at this function,

00:32:40.390 --> 00:32:43.510
it returns features.

00:32:43.510 --> 00:32:46.140
But in fact, it does not.

00:32:46.140 --> 00:32:49.050
What this function returns
is an operation node

00:32:49.050 --> 00:32:51.120
in the computation graph.

00:32:51.120 --> 00:32:55.590
And then later on when
this node is evaluated,

00:32:55.590 --> 00:32:57.640
it will return next
batch of examples

00:32:57.640 --> 00:32:58.890
several times in several ways.

00:32:58.890 --> 00:33:00.681
And this is a very
important, subtle point,

00:33:00.681 --> 00:33:03.360
so I have a separate
slide just for this.

00:33:03.360 --> 00:33:06.040
Because it's a key
point to understand.

00:33:06.040 --> 00:33:12.540
TensorFlow programs
in graph mode.

00:33:12.540 --> 00:33:15.360
In graph mode they
have two phases.

00:33:15.360 --> 00:33:18.030
In phase one, you construct
the computation graph,

00:33:18.030 --> 00:33:21.780
but no data is flowing
through the graph yet.

00:33:21.780 --> 00:33:24.730
Then in phase two, you
execute the computation

00:33:24.730 --> 00:33:26.820
within the TensorFlow session.

00:33:26.820 --> 00:33:30.040
And this is when data actually
flows through the graph.

00:33:30.040 --> 00:33:35.430
So in our dataset input
function, as it's implemented,

00:33:35.430 --> 00:33:37.840
it doesn't return any
batches of data yet.

00:33:37.840 --> 00:33:40.890
But instead what it does, it
adds nodes to the TensorFlow

00:33:40.890 --> 00:33:42.630
computation graph.

00:33:42.630 --> 00:33:46.620
And then, when
later on the nodes

00:33:46.620 --> 00:33:48.630
are related within
a session, they

00:33:48.630 --> 00:33:51.810
will be returning next
batch of training samples

00:33:51.810 --> 00:33:53.268
upon each evaluation.

00:33:56.540 --> 00:33:58.320
So we're back to
dataset input function.

00:33:58.320 --> 00:34:00.200
It returns features and labels.

00:34:00.200 --> 00:34:03.560
So features is an operation
node in a TensorFlow graph.

00:34:03.560 --> 00:34:07.160
And labels is another operation
node in a TensorFlow graph.

00:34:10.949 --> 00:34:11.760
Training the model.

00:34:11.760 --> 00:34:14.760
First, we're going to look at
how to train the model locally.

00:34:17.370 --> 00:34:22.010
So the model will be training
on examples we feed it.

00:34:22.010 --> 00:34:26.179
And with some number of
examples, some of which

00:34:26.179 --> 00:34:27.794
it trains, hopefully
it will learn how

00:34:27.794 --> 00:34:28.960
to make correct predictions.

00:34:28.960 --> 00:34:34.260
That how supervised
loading works.

00:34:34.260 --> 00:34:37.935
So the first step is to
construct a DNNRegressor.

00:34:37.935 --> 00:34:42.770
TensorFlow has several
built-in estimators.

00:34:42.770 --> 00:34:47.230
So a DNNRegressor is
one of the estimators,

00:34:47.230 --> 00:34:49.429
and it's intended
for use with models

00:34:49.429 --> 00:34:51.949
that make numerical
predictions, which is what

00:34:51.949 --> 00:34:54.710
we're doing in our example.

00:34:54.710 --> 00:34:58.040
Hidden units specifies
the structure

00:34:58.040 --> 00:34:59.780
of the neural network.

00:34:59.780 --> 00:35:01.940
So here, we are
going to be using

00:35:01.940 --> 00:35:04.310
two fully connected layers.

00:35:04.310 --> 00:35:08.150
And each layer is going to
have 128 hidden weights.

00:35:10.710 --> 00:35:13.201
Feature columns-- so this is
exactly the feature columns

00:35:13.201 --> 00:35:14.200
that we defined earlier.

00:35:14.200 --> 00:35:18.420
It's a list of specifications
of the dimensionality

00:35:18.420 --> 00:35:20.025
and type of each input feature.

00:35:23.070 --> 00:35:25.800
Model directory is in the
form of an argument that

00:35:25.800 --> 00:35:28.230
specifies where
the model is going

00:35:28.230 --> 00:35:33.910
to be saving its periodic
checkpoint, so its state.

00:35:33.910 --> 00:35:36.930
And where it's also going
to periodically saving

00:35:36.930 --> 00:35:42.410
its training and
evaluation statistics.

00:35:42.410 --> 00:35:45.500
So here, I'm again going
over the feature columns.

00:35:45.500 --> 00:35:47.310
There is embedding
column for the RIC,

00:35:47.310 --> 00:35:49.810
there is a numeric column for
interval sequence number shape

00:35:49.810 --> 00:35:55.070
one, sum interval in shape
one, low current mid--

00:35:55.070 --> 00:35:56.150
they all have width one.

00:35:56.150 --> 00:36:01.016
And delta log mids
has width 120.

00:36:01.016 --> 00:36:02.640
And I already said
that model directors

00:36:02.640 --> 00:36:05.400
is where the model
stores it's checkpoints.

00:36:05.400 --> 00:36:07.680
And this is also
importantly the directory

00:36:07.680 --> 00:36:12.060
from which the model reads
it's state initialized

00:36:12.060 --> 00:36:16.850
when you construct
DNNRegressor later on.

00:36:16.850 --> 00:36:20.470
And by using this feature,
you can train the model

00:36:20.470 --> 00:36:23.490
iteratively or incrementally.

00:36:23.490 --> 00:36:26.420
So the way it works is you
initialize DNNRegressor

00:36:26.420 --> 00:36:28.700
from the latest checkpoint.

00:36:28.700 --> 00:36:31.840
Then a train model on newly
available data, for example,

00:36:31.840 --> 00:36:34.290
or newly available dataset.

00:36:34.290 --> 00:36:37.562
And your checkpoints will
be automatically saved

00:36:37.562 --> 00:36:38.520
in the model directory.

00:36:38.520 --> 00:36:41.210
So it will create
new checkpoints

00:36:41.210 --> 00:36:45.470
with files under new file
names and automatically garbage

00:36:45.470 --> 00:36:48.910
collect old checkpoints.

00:36:48.910 --> 00:36:51.130
So for example, in a
case of financial data,

00:36:51.130 --> 00:36:52.940
you can incrementally
train the model

00:36:52.940 --> 00:36:56.260
at the end of each trading date.

00:36:56.260 --> 00:36:57.740
So at the end of
the trading date,

00:36:57.740 --> 00:37:00.510
you collect all market
data for this date.

00:37:00.510 --> 00:37:03.490
You train the model on training
examples for that date.

00:37:03.490 --> 00:37:05.230
The model starts
with a checkpoint

00:37:05.230 --> 00:37:06.630
from the previous date.

00:37:06.630 --> 00:37:11.170
After it trains, it stays in
the checkpoint representing

00:37:11.170 --> 00:37:14.650
its state after it has
incrementally trained

00:37:14.650 --> 00:37:15.859
the market data from today.

00:37:15.859 --> 00:37:17.150
And then tomorrow it can go on.

00:37:19.790 --> 00:37:21.660
So how to train the model.

00:37:21.660 --> 00:37:23.570
One thing before
we get to training

00:37:23.570 --> 00:37:29.310
is you may want to add some
custom performance metrics--

00:37:29.310 --> 00:37:30.480
evaluation metrics.

00:37:30.480 --> 00:37:34.930
By default, DNNRegressor
has one metric.

00:37:34.930 --> 00:37:38.610
It shows L2 loss values,
which is mean squared error.

00:37:38.610 --> 00:37:41.751
And it may be useful to have
root mean squared error,

00:37:41.751 --> 00:37:42.250
as well.

00:37:42.250 --> 00:37:47.540
So this is how you add the
custom evaluation metrics.

00:37:47.540 --> 00:37:49.560
So we're almost
at the point where

00:37:49.560 --> 00:37:51.464
we're training the models.

00:37:51.464 --> 00:37:52.880
Before we train
the model, we need

00:37:52.880 --> 00:37:56.550
to construct training
spec and evaluation spec.

00:37:56.550 --> 00:37:57.550
And what is that?

00:37:57.550 --> 00:38:02.260
It's to primarily
specify input functions.

00:38:02.260 --> 00:38:05.500
So we can use the
same input function,

00:38:05.500 --> 00:38:08.380
but simply initialize
them differently

00:38:08.380 --> 00:38:10.320
with a different list of files.

00:38:10.320 --> 00:38:12.820
We pass the list
of training files

00:38:12.820 --> 00:38:14.770
into the trained
specification, and we

00:38:14.770 --> 00:38:17.380
pass the list of
evaluation files

00:38:17.380 --> 00:38:20.260
for the eval specification.

00:38:20.260 --> 00:38:24.880
And very importantly,
when training models,

00:38:24.880 --> 00:38:28.900
evaluation data should start
after the end of the training

00:38:28.900 --> 00:38:29.400
date.

00:38:29.400 --> 00:38:34.040
You don't want to have
any look-aheads here.

00:38:34.040 --> 00:38:36.140
And also, it's important
to specify the number

00:38:36.140 --> 00:38:37.730
of maximum steps for training.

00:38:37.730 --> 00:38:40.670
Because depending on how you
implement your dataset input

00:38:40.670 --> 00:38:42.710
function, if you
don't specify these,

00:38:42.710 --> 00:38:45.050
your model may train forever.

00:38:45.050 --> 00:38:47.470
It will never stop.

00:38:47.470 --> 00:38:49.420
And finally, train
and evaluate function

00:38:49.420 --> 00:38:54.560
is the recommended way
of training a TensorFlow

00:38:54.560 --> 00:38:57.250
model that uses DNNRegressor.

00:38:57.250 --> 00:38:58.779
What it does, it
trains the model,

00:38:58.779 --> 00:39:00.320
it periodically
checkpoints the state

00:39:00.320 --> 00:39:06.450
and it periodically checkpoints
training and evaluation

00:39:06.450 --> 00:39:09.710
performance metrics, all
in the same directory.

00:39:09.710 --> 00:39:11.210
But there is a
separate subdirectory

00:39:11.210 --> 00:39:14.880
for evaluation metrics.

00:39:14.880 --> 00:39:18.030
Now, we had a look at how
to train the model locally.

00:39:18.030 --> 00:39:21.000
Now let's take a look at how
you will do it with the Cloud ML

00:39:21.000 --> 00:39:21.826
Engine.

00:39:21.826 --> 00:39:23.700
So why would you want
to use Cloud ML Engine?

00:39:23.700 --> 00:39:28.705
The primary purpose is
it gives us scalability.

00:39:28.705 --> 00:39:30.330
It gives the ability
to train the model

00:39:30.330 --> 00:39:33.940
with multiple workers, multiple
tasks at the same time.

00:39:33.940 --> 00:39:37.860
And this way you can train
your model many times faster

00:39:37.860 --> 00:39:42.690
and you can have a much faster
turnover over different verses

00:39:42.690 --> 00:39:44.330
for the models you want to try.

00:39:44.330 --> 00:39:48.290
You can do your research
much faster this way.

00:39:48.290 --> 00:39:53.420
So the good thing is, when
you transition your model

00:39:53.420 --> 00:39:57.290
from local training to
training with Cloud ML Engine,

00:39:57.290 --> 00:39:59.540
you don't need to
change your code at all.

00:39:59.540 --> 00:40:01.520
When you're using
estimators, they already

00:40:01.520 --> 00:40:04.160
have built-in support
for Cloud ML Engine.

00:40:04.160 --> 00:40:05.720
And that's extremely convenient.

00:40:05.720 --> 00:40:09.170
So all you have to do is
specify a configuration file,

00:40:09.170 --> 00:40:11.870
you need to specify code to
construct training package--

00:40:11.870 --> 00:40:14.650
the setup-- and you need
to provide the main program

00:40:14.650 --> 00:40:16.400
to train the model,
which you already have

00:40:16.400 --> 00:40:19.100
because it's the same program.

00:40:19.100 --> 00:40:21.240
So configuration settings.

00:40:21.240 --> 00:40:24.209
You need to specify
the scale tier.

00:40:24.209 --> 00:40:25.750
So in this example,
we're going to be

00:40:25.750 --> 00:40:28.902
using a custom tier because
it's the most interesting--

00:40:28.902 --> 00:40:29.485
most flexible.

00:40:29.485 --> 00:40:34.940
You can specify exactly
the hardware configuration

00:40:34.940 --> 00:40:39.840
for different types of
nodes in your training job.

00:40:39.840 --> 00:40:42.600
So let's take a look at the
different types of nodes.

00:40:42.600 --> 00:40:43.830
There are three types.

00:40:43.830 --> 00:40:48.240
There are worker nodes,
which calculate gradients.

00:40:48.240 --> 00:40:51.750
And workers are
the nodes that do

00:40:51.750 --> 00:40:55.590
lots of numerical computations,
such as matrix multiplications.

00:40:55.590 --> 00:40:57.510
And they are the
nodes that you will

00:40:57.510 --> 00:41:02.170
want to run with GPUs to
get faster performance.

00:41:02.170 --> 00:41:04.050
Then there are
parameter server nodes,

00:41:04.050 --> 00:41:06.870
which update parameters with
gradient vectors from workers.

00:41:06.870 --> 00:41:11.140
And then there is a master
node, which coordinates everyone

00:41:11.140 --> 00:41:13.720
and also operates as a worker.

00:41:13.720 --> 00:41:16.320
So based on that
usage, we are going

00:41:16.320 --> 00:41:21.990
to be using standard GPU
for master and worker nodes.

00:41:21.990 --> 00:41:24.340
And we're going to be using
certain CPU for parameter

00:41:24.340 --> 00:41:25.470
servers.

00:41:25.470 --> 00:41:28.380
And we'll also get to specify
how many workers or how many

00:41:28.380 --> 00:41:29.850
parameter servers
we want to use.

00:41:29.850 --> 00:41:31.940
For this example, we're
going to be training model

00:41:31.940 --> 00:41:36.350
with eight workers and
four parameter servers.

00:41:36.350 --> 00:41:38.927
The code to construct
training package--

00:41:38.927 --> 00:41:40.760
there is a separate
file we need to provide.

00:41:40.760 --> 00:41:43.640
And you specify the lists
of required packages.

00:41:43.640 --> 00:41:45.980
In many cases, this list
will be actually empty,

00:41:45.980 --> 00:41:50.870
because each Cloud ML
Engine runtime comes

00:41:50.870 --> 00:41:58.350
with a already built-in list of
many popular Python packages.

00:41:58.350 --> 00:42:04.940
So if you are using something
that's very nonstandard,

00:42:04.940 --> 00:42:07.500
you may need to specify it
here in this file in setup.

00:42:07.500 --> 00:42:10.850
But in many cases,
we won't need to.

00:42:10.850 --> 00:42:13.280
And the main program
to train the model

00:42:13.280 --> 00:42:16.340
is the same as for
local training.

00:42:16.340 --> 00:42:18.110
And again, let's go
over what it does.

00:42:18.110 --> 00:42:20.260
It parses command
line parameters,

00:42:20.260 --> 00:42:23.220
it defines feature columns,
it constructs DNNRegressor,

00:42:23.220 --> 00:42:26.946
it defines evaluation
metric if you need any,

00:42:26.946 --> 00:42:29.770
it defines the input function
that will be reading TFRecords

00:42:29.770 --> 00:42:32.200
from a list of
files, and it will

00:42:32.200 --> 00:42:35.830
start training and
evaluating the model.

00:42:35.830 --> 00:42:39.100
So this is the command they
use to submit a job to Cloud ML

00:42:39.100 --> 00:42:40.530
Engine.

00:42:40.530 --> 00:42:42.710
You need to specify
a unique job name.

00:42:42.710 --> 00:42:44.922
You specify the path
to your main program

00:42:44.922 --> 00:42:45.880
that does the training.

00:42:45.880 --> 00:42:48.430
This is the program that
creates DNNRegressor and calls

00:42:48.430 --> 00:42:51.270
the train and evaluate function.

00:42:51.270 --> 00:42:54.390
You specify the path to
your configuration file.

00:42:54.390 --> 00:42:56.390
You specify the
path of your staging

00:42:56.390 --> 00:42:59.271
bucket in cloud storage.

00:42:59.271 --> 00:43:00.770
And you specify the
runtime version.

00:43:00.770 --> 00:43:03.030
So this number in
the runtime version--

00:43:03.030 --> 00:43:06.750
runtime version is exactly
the version of TensorFlow.

00:43:06.750 --> 00:43:11.070
So depending on what version of
TensorFlow you intend to use,

00:43:11.070 --> 00:43:13.650
depending on your
code dependencies,

00:43:13.650 --> 00:43:15.780
you will specify
the version here.

00:43:15.780 --> 00:43:20.010
And as I said, each runtime
version of Cloud ML Engine

00:43:20.010 --> 00:43:23.520
comes with a different
list of packages

00:43:23.520 --> 00:43:26.360
already built-in the runtime.

00:43:26.360 --> 00:43:27.860
Then you need to
specify the region.

00:43:27.860 --> 00:43:30.600
There are two region support
at the moment, US central one

00:43:30.600 --> 00:43:33.010
and US east one.

00:43:33.010 --> 00:43:34.980
And finally, if
you need to you can

00:43:34.980 --> 00:43:37.140
specify a list of
custom parameters

00:43:37.140 --> 00:43:41.880
that will be passed to
your training application.

00:43:41.880 --> 00:43:44.490
Evaluation of the model.

00:43:44.490 --> 00:43:46.226
So what is evaluation?

00:43:46.226 --> 00:43:47.600
The key question
I want to answer

00:43:47.600 --> 00:43:49.770
is, how precise is our model?

00:43:49.770 --> 00:43:51.960
What's the quality
of predictions?

00:43:51.960 --> 00:43:54.050
How well does it perform
on evaluation data?

00:43:54.050 --> 00:43:56.350
So we're going to be using
TensorBoard for this.

00:43:56.350 --> 00:43:57.950
This is how you
run a TensorBoard

00:43:57.950 --> 00:44:00.290
and you command this code,
TensorBoard, and then

00:44:00.290 --> 00:44:05.900
you specify the directory
where model checkpoints are.

00:44:05.900 --> 00:44:08.120
And tabulation statistics
and training statistics

00:44:08.120 --> 00:44:08.861
are the same.

00:44:08.861 --> 00:44:10.610
This is the same model
directory that they

00:44:10.610 --> 00:44:15.800
used when they constructed
DNNRegressor object.

00:44:15.800 --> 00:44:22.730
So this is what L2 loss
looks like on the training

00:44:22.730 --> 00:44:26.794
data that were used
for this model example.

00:44:26.794 --> 00:44:29.210
This is what the loss looks
like for the first 3 and a 1/2

00:44:29.210 --> 00:44:29.960
hours of training.

00:44:29.960 --> 00:44:35.150
You can see that L2 loss
started around 90 probably,

00:44:35.150 --> 00:44:37.711
and it was gradually decreasing
over the next 2 and a 1/2

00:44:37.711 --> 00:44:38.210
hours.

00:44:38.210 --> 00:44:41.250
Meaning that as
the model trains,

00:44:41.250 --> 00:44:44.000
its loss on training
data becomes

00:44:44.000 --> 00:44:45.440
gradual lower and lower.

00:44:45.440 --> 00:44:47.270
But this doesn't
necessarily mean

00:44:47.270 --> 00:44:50.870
that the model is becoming
better in its predictions.

00:44:50.870 --> 00:44:52.520
Because what we
really care about

00:44:52.520 --> 00:44:55.250
is the quote of generalizations.

00:44:55.250 --> 00:44:58.675
And when we look at the L2
loss for evaluation data,

00:44:58.675 --> 00:45:03.350
we see that the model
starts around 206

00:45:03.350 --> 00:45:04.670
after 30 minutes of training.

00:45:04.670 --> 00:45:07.880
And then actually, you can see
that the loss from evaluation

00:45:07.880 --> 00:45:12.470
data increases slightly
to around 210 to 211.

00:45:12.470 --> 00:45:14.510
So what this means
is you probably

00:45:14.510 --> 00:45:17.780
want to stop training your model
after the first half an hour.

00:45:17.780 --> 00:45:21.650
Because after that, L2
loss on training data

00:45:21.650 --> 00:45:22.730
will keep decreasing.

00:45:22.730 --> 00:45:24.680
But this simply means
that the model gradually

00:45:24.680 --> 00:45:25.620
starts overfeeding.

00:45:25.620 --> 00:45:27.620
So you want to stop
training, because you're not

00:45:27.620 --> 00:45:30.289
going to improve quality
of predictions after that.

00:45:30.289 --> 00:45:32.330
The longer it trains after
the first half an hour

00:45:32.330 --> 00:45:35.720
in this example, the
worse actually the model

00:45:35.720 --> 00:45:36.960
predicts evaluation data.

00:45:36.960 --> 00:45:39.360
So there is usually
an optimal amount

00:45:39.360 --> 00:45:40.610
of training you want to apply.

00:45:43.530 --> 00:45:46.050
So I said this already.

00:45:46.050 --> 00:45:47.700
Let's take a look at R squared.

00:45:47.700 --> 00:45:49.460
So as I mentioned
before, square gives you

00:45:49.460 --> 00:45:53.090
an idea of how well the model
performs on evaluation data

00:45:53.090 --> 00:45:57.560
compared to a trivial model that
always predicts zero returns.

00:45:57.560 --> 00:46:00.110
And as I said, if
the value is zero,

00:46:00.110 --> 00:46:01.710
it means the model is worthless.

00:46:01.710 --> 00:46:04.140
If it's one, it means
the model is perfect.

00:46:04.140 --> 00:46:07.380
And usually, we expect
the value to be closer

00:46:07.380 --> 00:46:09.200
to zero than the one,
but to be positive.

00:46:09.200 --> 00:46:11.550
And as it happens,
that's the case here.

00:46:11.550 --> 00:46:16.350
R squared is approximately
0.10 to 0.11 after the first 20

00:46:16.350 --> 00:46:20.190
to 30 minutes of training, and
then it declines to a range

00:46:20.190 --> 00:46:26.230
between 0.08 and 0.09, which
is actually decent for when

00:46:26.230 --> 00:46:31.050
you're predicting
five-minute intraday prices.

00:46:31.050 --> 00:46:34.260
But remember, this
was based on model

00:46:34.260 --> 00:46:37.980
trained on only 16
days of market data

00:46:37.980 --> 00:46:40.290
and evaluated on
only one day of data.

00:46:40.290 --> 00:46:45.150
So this is fine for an example
of how to build the model

00:46:45.150 --> 00:46:48.030
and how to train
it, but this is not

00:46:48.030 --> 00:46:51.730
what you would want to do
for actual use in production.

00:46:51.730 --> 00:46:54.085
For use in production
training, you

00:46:54.085 --> 00:46:56.460
would want to train your model
with several years of data

00:46:56.460 --> 00:46:58.043
and evaluate on
several years of data.

00:47:00.480 --> 00:47:01.470
Generating forecasts.

00:47:01.470 --> 00:47:06.210
Once we have the model, how
do we generate forecasts?

00:47:06.210 --> 00:47:13.310
For training, it's convenient to
save data into TFRecord files.

00:47:13.310 --> 00:47:16.650
When you want to build
a forecast in real-time,

00:47:16.650 --> 00:47:20.790
it may actually be more
convenient to store data

00:47:20.790 --> 00:47:25.710
in pandas dataframes from
memory than to read from files.

00:47:25.710 --> 00:47:28.110
So I will show how
to do it a bit later.

00:47:28.110 --> 00:47:30.440
So the key is that for
generating predictions,

00:47:30.440 --> 00:47:34.620
you use the same
initialization settings as when

00:47:34.620 --> 00:47:37.980
you were training the model.

00:47:37.980 --> 00:47:40.130
Same hidden units,
same model directories,

00:47:40.130 --> 00:47:41.120
same feature columns.

00:47:41.120 --> 00:47:42.790
That's important.

00:47:45.380 --> 00:47:49.070
As I said, we're going to
be using pandas dataframe.

00:47:49.070 --> 00:47:54.420
And when input functions based
on the pandas dataframe--

00:47:54.420 --> 00:47:58.250
so each dataframe row is going
to include feature values

00:47:58.250 --> 00:47:59.900
for training example.

00:47:59.900 --> 00:48:06.470
And each column is going to have
feature values for one feature

00:48:06.470 --> 00:48:07.130
type.

00:48:07.130 --> 00:48:11.990
And the input function is going
return a dictionary, mapping

00:48:11.990 --> 00:48:13.620
names of input
features to tensors

00:48:13.620 --> 00:48:16.910
containing the feature's
values for the entire batch.

00:48:16.910 --> 00:48:21.452
So essentially, values for one
column in the pandas dataframe.

00:48:21.452 --> 00:48:23.410
So the feature tensors
will be two-dimensional.

00:48:23.410 --> 00:48:25.760
The first dimension is
the size of the batch,

00:48:25.760 --> 00:48:28.430
which is the number of
rows in the dataframe.

00:48:28.430 --> 00:48:30.470
And the second
dimension is the width

00:48:30.470 --> 00:48:33.030
of the corresponding
feature column.

00:48:33.030 --> 00:48:38.140
So we have RIC, we have
interval sequence number, some

00:48:38.140 --> 00:48:42.480
of interval volumes, log of
the current mid, delta log mid.

00:48:42.480 --> 00:48:46.060
So here, the second
dimension is 120.

00:48:46.060 --> 00:48:49.000
And there is a little bit
different transformation here,

00:48:49.000 --> 00:48:51.450
because first, we need to
convert the list of lists

00:48:51.450 --> 00:48:54.670
into numpy
multidimensional array.

00:48:54.670 --> 00:48:56.560
And then we shape the
array as required,

00:48:56.560 --> 00:49:00.370
with the second
dimension set to 120.

00:49:00.370 --> 00:49:03.730
Then we use the predict method
that returns predictions

00:49:03.730 --> 00:49:06.380
generator.

00:49:06.380 --> 00:49:09.190
We use islice method
to get the iterator.

00:49:09.190 --> 00:49:12.970
And here, it's important to
specify the number of steps

00:49:12.970 --> 00:49:13.690
in the iterator.

00:49:13.690 --> 00:49:18.350
Otherwise, it may never stop.

00:49:18.350 --> 00:49:20.870
And then as we iterate,
we get this list

00:49:20.870 --> 00:49:26.520
of predictions which correspond
to rows in the dataframe.

00:49:26.520 --> 00:49:28.010
So the length of
this list will be

00:49:28.010 --> 00:49:31.870
exactly the length of the
dataframe-- the number of rows.

00:49:31.870 --> 00:49:32.930
So the wrap-up.

00:49:32.930 --> 00:49:35.840
What have we accomplished?

00:49:35.840 --> 00:49:41.540
We have shown how to build model
features using Thomson Reuters

00:49:41.540 --> 00:49:45.660
data in Google Cloud
using BigQuery.

00:49:45.660 --> 00:49:50.750
We have looked at how serialize
input features in TFRecord

00:49:50.750 --> 00:49:53.900
files in Cloud Storage.

00:49:53.900 --> 00:50:00.230
We have seen how to construct
DNNRegressors and build

00:50:00.230 --> 00:50:02.210
and train the models locally.

00:50:02.210 --> 00:50:05.180
And we have seen how
to train the model

00:50:05.180 --> 00:50:08.440
as a scalable distributed
application using Cloud ML

00:50:08.440 --> 00:50:12.400
Engine with multiple workers.

00:50:12.400 --> 00:50:18.940
And now we invite you to apply
the knowledge that you have

00:50:18.940 --> 00:50:22.900
gained from the session
to try and build and train

00:50:22.900 --> 00:50:26.020
your own machine learning
model in Google Cloud,

00:50:26.020 --> 00:50:29.470
and put all pieces
of your own puzzles

00:50:29.470 --> 00:50:32.770
together into a
beautiful picture.

00:50:32.770 --> 00:50:33.310
Thank you.

00:50:33.310 --> 00:50:36.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

